Yes: **eventually the harness itself becomes part of the WorkerGenome**. But I would not make Moltwork dynamically choose among ten frameworks yet. First establish a small Pareto frontier of 3–4 execution profiles, prove the measurements are correct, and only then let BATS/Hydra route between them.

The current stack should stay centered on Letta. Your existing design explicitly defined the narrow proof as persistent Letta worker → training trajectories → Hydra context → validated memory/skill mutation → held-out improvement.  And Letta's new Agents SDK is specifically positioning itself around persistent identity, long-term experience, memory and cross-machine state. ([Letta][1])

PydanticAI is the one challenger I would integrate immediately, because it now overlaps with a surprising amount of what we need: persistent Memory, Skills, planning, subagents, step persistence, durable execution, provider-adaptive thinking, spend limits, per-response/model costs, OpenTelemetry instrumentation, and Code Mode for collapsing many tool operations into one model round-trip. ([Pydantic][2])

### The initial stack

| Layer                  | Use                                              |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| **WorkerKit**          | canonical work/economic contract                 |
| **Harbor**             | environments, trials, verifiers, reproducibility |
| **Letta**              | primary persistent-learning harness              |
| **PydanticAI Harness** | composable/budget-observable challenger          |
| **Direct**             | cheapest one-shot baseline                       |
| **Hermes**             | off-the-shelf external learning baseline         |
| **HydraDB**            | empirical organizational memory                  |
| **BATS**               | runtime compute/resource allocator               |
| **GEPA**               | offline WorkerGenome evolution                   |
| **Trace2Skill**        | batch trajectory → skill evolution               |
| **Letta Evals**        | Letta-specific memory/state regression tests     |

That is enough. **Do not integrate GBrain now.** GBrain is essentially an agent-readable knowledge/memory system exposed through CLI/MCP; that overlaps heavily with the Hydra organizational-memory role rather than giving us a meaningfully different execution harness. ([GitHub][3])

Hermes is easier: Harbor already lists `hermes` among its built-in installed agents. We can benchmark it without making Hermes foundational to Moltwork. ([GitHub][4])

## Upstreams the coding agent should pin

These are the repos worth having locally as reference implementations. Don't casually copy them wholesale; pin exact commits in `upstreams.lock.json`.

* [Harbor](https://github.com/harbor-framework/harbor?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — cross-harness trials/worlds/evaluation. Harbor supports arbitrary external/custom agents and produces reproducible trials. ([GitHub][5])
* [Harbor Cookbook](https://github.com/harbor-framework/harbor-cookbook?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — runnable patterns.
* [Pydantic AI](https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — typed core agent loop. ([GitHub][6])
* [Pydantic AI Harness](https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai-harness?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — Coder, Memory, planning, context controls, budgets, etc. ([GitHub][7])
* [Letta Agent SDK](https://github.com/letta-ai/letta-agent-sdk?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — current stateful-agent interface.
* [Letta Evals](https://github.com/letta-ai/letta-evals?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — stateful-agent evaluation framework. ([GitHub][8])
