# Immediate execution order

1. Create `mwgym`.
2. Lock WorkerKit and upstream commits.
3. Port existing MWLab schema/Harbor parser pieces.
4. Implement canonical WorkerGenome.
5. Implement ModelCall/ToolCall/Decision telemetry.
6. Fix Letta config/telemetry.
7. Wire Direct adapter.
8. Wire PydanticAI adapter.
9. Wire all three into Harbor.
10. Build deterministic filesystem benchmark.
11. Execute Experiment 001.
12. Debug measurements until reconciled.
13. Run full crossover suite.
14. Build memory-dependent dataset.
15. Execute Experiment 002.
16. Add BATS.
17. Execute Experiment 003.
18. Add Trace2Skill checkpoint learning.
19. Add GEPA genome evolution.
20. Only then implement YGO World 001.

The project is not ready for YGO until we can trust every number emitted by a simple filesystem experiment.

The key architectural decision is therefore **not** “Pydantic or Letta?”. It is:

> **Letta is our primary hypothesis for durable learning; Pydantic is the strongest lighter/composable control; Direct is the economic floor; Hermes is an off-the-shelf external baseline. Harbor makes them comparable.**

That preserves everything useful from the Letta plan instead of throwing it away. Your existing `LEARNING_LAB.md` had already converged on “Letta = worker, Hydra = lab brain, cg = scientist.”  Harbor then gives us the neutral execution/evaluation substrate that lets us challenge the **Letta = worker** assumption scientifically rather than ideologically. Your Harbor design already anticipated precisely this persistent-worker/ephemeral-world split.

And Pydantic is especially worth testing because it may reveal something quite useful: **you might end up with Letta for workers that genuinely need accumulated identity/experience, Pydantic Code Mode for high-throughput coding/action runs, and Direct for obvious cheap actions**. MWGym then learns the crossover boundaries instead of us guessing them.

